Now I have the full picture. Your `ReportGenerator` is on .NET 10, so the built-in `TimeProvider` is the right abstraction for `DateTime.UtcNow` — no custom wrapper needed.

Let me also look at the other statics in that class to give you complete guidance.

The `ReportGenerator` uses these statics:

| Static | Abstraction |
|---|---|
| `DateTime.UtcNow` | `TimeProvider` (built-in .NET 8+) |
| `Environment.*` | Custom `IEnvironmentProvider` |
| `Directory.*`, `File.*`, `Path.*` | `System.IO.Abstractions` (NuGet) |
| `Console.WriteLine` | Custom `IConsole` |

You asked specifically about **time**, so I'll focus there. Here's what to do:

**1. Register `TimeProvider` in DI** — add to `Program.cs`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**2. Inject it into `ReportGenerator`:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        // ...
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {timeProvider.GetUtcNow():O}\n...";
        // ...
    }
}
```

**3. Test with `FakeTimeProvider`:**

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 10, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);

var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");
// timestamp is now deterministic: "20260710_120000"
```

No custom interface or wrapper class needed — `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 10. The remaining statics (`File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Environment.*`, `Console.*`) would each need their own abstractions when you're ready to tackle those.
